-- | Federico's first objection, answered on the ledger instead of in a hash.
--
-- THE OBJECTION. On forum.canton.network/t/9114 a Canton Community Tech
-- Partner said the hash chain makes existing receipts tamper-evident but
-- "doesn't prove that a receipt wasn't omitted". Refusal.daml does not fix
-- that. It makes a refusal that HAPPENED impossible to invent, delete,
-- backdate or reorder, and says so in its own footer. It is silent about an
-- attempt that was never recorded at all.
--
-- The receipt-chain answer in SPEC 6c is weaker than it sounds: an outcomes
-- digest catches a refusal RELABELLED at disclosure time, but a producer who
-- never wrote the entry computes a digest over the shorter list and it
-- verifies. No commitment scheme reaches write-time omission, because the
-- producer chooses what to commit to.
--
-- WHAT ACTUALLY REACHES IT. Stop trying to prove a negative about records and
-- publish a POSITIVE number instead. One contract per (owner, actor) holds a
-- count of every attempt that has passed through it. The auditor is an
-- observer of that contract, so the auditor reads the count directly from the
-- ledger rather than from a disclosure the operator assembled.
--
-- The auditor can then do arithmetic the operator cannot interfere with:
--
--     attempts = 50, refusals = [3, 7, 19]
--     records handed over = 2
--     -> one refusal is missing, and its position is 3, 7 or 19.
--
-- Note what the auditor still CANNOT see: the 47 attempts that were allowed.
-- Not what they were, not who they were to, not for how much. The count is
-- disclosed, the content is not. That is the whole shape of the thing. An
-- auditor who must take the operator's word for the DENOMINATOR cannot audit
-- a sample, and a transparent ledger that discloses the numerator as well is
-- not usable by a regulated firm.
--
-- HOW AN APPLICATION WIRES IT. The application holds the journal's contract
-- id and exercises it in the SAME transaction as the work:
--
--     choice Withdraw : ...
--       with journal : ContractId AttemptJournal
--       do case refusalReason ... of
--            Some rule -> do
--              _ <- exercise journal Refuse with
--                     action = "withdraw"; detail = show amount; rule
--              pure ()
--            None -> do
--              _ <- exercise journal Allow with action = "withdraw"
--              -- the real work, here, inside the same transaction
--
-- Both branches consume and recreate the journal, so both increment. Daml
-- commits a transaction whole or not at all, which is the entire load-bearing
-- fact: the work and the increment cannot come apart. Skipping the increment
-- means skipping the work.
--
-- NO CONTRACT KEY, and this is a Daml 3 fact rather than a preference. Daml-LF
-- 2.1 removed contract keys: `daml build` on this package rejects a `key`
-- clause outright with "this feature is not currently supported". So there is
-- no way to make the ledger refuse a SECOND journal for the same pair.
--
-- Uniqueness is therefore DETECTED and not prevented, which is the same
-- footing as everything else here. The auditor is an observer of every journal
-- they are named on, so a second journal opened to run attempts past a clean
-- first one appears in the auditor's own contract set without anyone telling
-- them. An operator who opens one has created the evidence against themselves.
-- An auditor's first check is a count of live journals per pair, and test 8
-- is that check.
--
-- THE REVERT CASE, raised by Arpit Karnatak of Vacuumlabs on
-- forum.canton.network/t/9084 on 18 October 2026, and he is right. If the
-- check PASSES, the work proceeds, and then something later in the same
-- transaction reverts, Daml rolls the whole thing back and takes the count
-- with it. The attempt leaves nothing.
--
-- That is the other face of the property above. The work and the count cannot
-- come apart, which is exactly why they also die together. The trade-off is
-- real and has no free side:
--
--   same transaction as the work -> inseparable, but both vanish on a revert
--   its own transaction, first   -> survives the revert, but can now describe
--                                   work that never happened
--
-- The refuse branch does NOT have this problem, and the asymmetry is the
-- answer rather than an excuse. When the check says no, the RefusalRecord is
-- the only thing in that transaction. There is no later step that can revert
-- it. `testARefusalHasNothingLaterThatCanRevertIt` holds that, and
-- `testAnAllowedAttemptThatRevertsIsLost` holds the limit.
--
-- So the claim is narrow on purpose. This is a complete record of refusals
-- that were DECIDED. It is not a complete log of everything attempted, and
-- offchain logging remains the right tool for reverts. The difference is
-- incentive, not mechanism: nobody gains by hiding a crash, and somebody can
-- gain by losing a refusal. The second is the one that has to sit where it
-- cannot be quietly dropped.
--
-- WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN, and this is a design contract rather than a
-- theorem. This module cannot force an application to route its choices
-- through the journal. An application with a second, unjournalled path to the
-- same asset has a hole, and no module imported by that application can see
-- it. What this module changes is WHERE the hole has to be: it moves from
-- "any attempt, invisibly" to "a code path an auditor can look for", and the
-- count makes the journalled path's completeness checkable rather than
-- asserted. Archiving the journal to reset the count is itself a ledger event
-- requiring both signatories.
module Journal where

import Refusal
import Attempt
import DA.Text qualified as T

-- | One per (owner, actor). Consumed and recreated by every attempt, so the
-- count is the ledger's and not the application's.
template AttemptJournal
  with
    owner : Party
      -- ^ Who is accountable. Signatory: a count nobody signs is a metric.
    actor : Party
      -- ^ Whose attempts are being counted. Also a signatory, so neither
      -- party can later claim the other inflated or deflated the number.
    guardian : Party
      -- ^ The party a guarded asset requires. A signatory here, so its
      -- authority flows into `Open` and onto the `Attempt` it creates.
      -- See Guarded.daml for what this does and what it does not do.
    auditor : Optional Party
      -- ^ Named once, in advance. This party reads the DENOMINATOR, and is a
      -- SIGNATORY rather than an observer. `None` discloses to nobody and
      -- gives up the uniqueness guarantee below.
      --
      -- PREDGE pointed this out on forum.canton.network/t/9114 on 21 October
      -- 2026, and it was the answer to a gap this file had already given up
      -- on. The previous note here said Daml-LF 2.1 removed contract keys so
      -- a second journal could only be DETECTED. Wrong. A create needs the
      -- authority of every signatory, so with the auditor on that line
      -- nobody can open a second journal without them. Prevention, with no
      -- keys.
      --
      -- It costs nothing at runtime. A choice that recreates this contract
      -- carries its signatories' authority already, so the auditor does not
      -- have to be online for an attempt. They are needed only to open a
      -- journal in the first place, which is the one moment you want them.
    attempts : Int
      -- ^ Every attempt that has passed through, allowed or refused. The
      -- number the auditor divides by.
    refusals : [Int]
      -- ^ The positions, in order, that were refused. The auditor's list of
      -- what they are entitled to be shown. Deliberately holds the position
      -- and NOT the reason: the reason lives on the RefusalRecord, which can
      -- be withheld. A withheld reason then leaves a position with no record
      -- against it, which is exactly the detection this module exists for.
  where
    -- The auditor is on the signatory line above, so no separate observer
    -- clause is needed: a signatory is already a stakeholder and sees it.
    signatory [owner, actor, guardian] <> optional [] (\a -> [a]) auditor

    -- A fresh journal starts at zero with nothing refused, and no later state
    -- can have fewer attempts than refusals.
    ensure attempts >= 0 && length refusals <= attempts

    -- | The rule said no. Numbers the attempt, writes the record, moves
    -- nothing. Returns the new journal so the caller cannot forget it.
    choice Refuse : (ContractId RefusalRecord, ContractId AttemptJournal)
      with
        action : Text
        detail : Text
        rule : Text
      controller owner, actor
      do
        now <- getTime
        let n = attempts + 1
        record <- create RefusalRecord with
          owner; actor; action; detail; rule
          refusedAt = now
          auditor
        journal <- create this with
          attempts = n
          refusals = refusals <> [n]
        pure (record, journal)

    -- | The rule said yes. Numbers the attempt and writes NOTHING about what
    -- it was. The application does its real work in this same transaction.
    --
    -- There is no RefusalRecord here and there is no AcceptanceRecord either.
    -- The auditor's view of an allowed attempt is that the count went up by
    -- one. That is the asymmetry the whole project is about, kept on purpose.
    choice Allow : ContractId AttemptJournal
      with
        action : Text
          -- ^ Taken and discarded. It exists so the call site reads the same
          -- in both branches and a reviewer can see the two are paired.
      controller owner, actor
      do create this with attempts = attempts + 1

    -- | Take the number FIRST, in a transaction that holds nothing else, and
    -- hand back an open attempt for the work to consume.
    --
    -- This is the answer to the revert case that `Allow` cannot handle. The
    -- count and the attempt commit here, alone, so no later failure can
    -- remove them. The work happens in the NEXT transaction, by exercising
    -- `Settled` or `Refused` on the attempt. If that transaction reverts, the
    -- attempt simply stays open, and an open attempt is the evidence.
    --
    -- Costs an extra round trip. Use `Allow` when the work cannot fail after
    -- the check, and this when it can.
    --
    -- Note `refusals` is NOT updated here, and cannot be: whether this
    -- attempt is refused is not known yet. In the two-phase flow the auditor
    -- reconciles `attempts` against the refusal records and the attempts
    -- still open, rather than reading a list off the journal.
    choice Open : (ContractId Attempt, ContractId AttemptJournal)
      with
        salt : Text
          -- ^ Fresh random value, generated by the caller and kept OFF the
          -- ledger until resolution. Without it the commitment binds but does
          -- not hide, and a payment action is low enough entropy to brute
          -- force. See `attemptCommitment`.
        action : Text
        detail : Text
      controller owner, actor
      do
        assertMsg "an attempt needs a salt, or the commitment does not hide"
          (T.length salt >= 32)
        now <- getTime
        let n = attempts + 1
        attempt <- create Attempt with
          owner; actor; guardian; auditor; n
          commitment = attemptCommitment salt action detail
          openedAt = now
        journal <- create this with attempts = n
        pure (attempt, journal)

    -- | What the auditor is entitled to, readable by the auditor without
    -- asking the operator for anything. Non-consuming: asking must not
    -- change the number being asked about.
    nonconsuming choice Expect : (Int, [Int])
      controller optional owner identity auditor
      do pure (attempts, refusals)

-- | Off-ledger arithmetic, here so both sides compute it the same way.
--
-- Given what the journal says and the positions actually handed over, the
-- positions that are missing. An empty result is the only passing answer.
-- Positions claimed that the journal never refused come back too, because a
-- fabricated record is the other half of the same lie.
missingRefusals : (Int, [Int]) -> [Int] -> [Int]
missingRefusals (_, expected) disclosed =
  filter (`notElem` disclosed) expected
    <> filter (`notElem` expected) disclosed
